#!/bin/sh
# Flow offloading: the feature, the packages and both options all stay - only the
# DEFAULT is OFF as of 2026-10-27. Turn it on from LuCI (Network -> Firewall ->
# General Settings -> "Software flow offloading" / "Hardware flow offloading") or:
#
#   uci set firewall.@defaults[0].flow_offloading='1'
#   uci set firewall.@defaults[0].flow_offloading_hw='1'    # needs the first one
#   uci commit firewall && fw4 restart
#
# What the options do (unchanged since v1.3): the gmac driver has the PPE
# hardware path (zx279128s-ppe.c, TC_SETUP_FT) and the image carries
# kmod-nft-offload, so with them fw4 installs an nft flowtable; with
# flow_offloading_hw the flow block is handed to the PPE and established IPv4
# TCP/UDP flows are forwarded without the CPU (v1.3 measured >=395 Mbit/s wired,
# up from the ~305/190 Mbit/s software ceiling). If the PPE refuses the block,
# fw4 falls back to the software flowtable.
#
# Why the default flipped: with flow_offloading_hw=1 the PPE's IPv6 TCP path is
# broken on this SoC - the handshake completes in ~1 ms and then not a single
# byte of payload moves. ICMPv6 is unaffected (never offloaded), IPv4 is fine,
# which is what makes it look like a random hang. Re-measured on hardware on
# 2026-10-27 against a freshly flashed image whose default was still 1 / 1:
#
#   flow_offloading=1 + flow_offloading_hw=1  -> IPv6 TCP: connect ok, 0 bytes, 6 s timeout
#   flow_offloading=1 + flow_offloading_hw=0  -> IPv6 TCP: HTTP/1.0 200 OK in 1 ms
#   flow_offloading=0 + flow_offloading_hw=0  -> same, works
#
# So the flowtable path is opt-in now, and the safe state is the shipped one.
# Both keys are written explicitly even though 0 is the stock firewall default:
# this script is the one place that states the policy, so an edit elsewhere
# cannot silently re-enable the broken path.
#
# Known limits if you do turn it on (carried over from the sibling ZX279133
# port, right.com.cn thread-8486503):
#  - PPPoE flows may not take the hardware path - if your WAN is PPPoE and
#    throughput looks software-bound, compare against a plain IP uplink;
#  - the PPE hash table is small (384 entries on this driver); overflow flows
#    fall back to software gracefully (driver counters: ppe_add_skip,
#    ppe_add_fail, under /sys/kernel/debug/92000000.ethernet/);
#  - IPv6 TCP will stall again - that is the PPE bug above, not a regression.
uci -q batch <<-EOF
	set firewall.@defaults[0].flow_offloading='0'
	set firewall.@defaults[0].flow_offloading_hw='0'
	commit firewall
EOF
exit 0
